[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9653f49-1a01-5acd-8ce6-f3cd8095fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387369} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3322db-d223-55b3-b7dc-f9fd758349fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387370} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c61a6c-a91d-50a4-a701-bf4e530a3094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387371} {:answer/id #uuid "d4be85c4-9c1a-5af1-b31a-957bb0b77474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387372} {:answer/id #uuid "95f050db-1db6-5212-8a8b-3de82028eac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4be85c4-9c1a-5af1-b31a-957bb0b77474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387372} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77fad7f-b6e3-5827-9df5-363a99a7be9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2a142a9-d042-56b2-ba86-b30a7fb0dfd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-123283" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=-3\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(-3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-3t\\,i+4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-3t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{-3t+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45471587-a7b6-5b1b-b719-d3191acd9214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387376} {:answer/id #uuid "71fd8321-f9a6-51e7-bfa3-7cfd909174cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡t+11}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387377} {:answer/id #uuid "e34c6854-26cf-5803-b417-8b39a6025039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387378} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1b61e2-d4d6-56cf-9d31-54bbcd9c6297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387379} {:answer/id #uuid "96bdd232-9416-51e7-8540-73037f2f89d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡t+11}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e34c6854-26cf-5803-b417-8b39a6025039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387378} :answer-field/id #uuid "60c78d9a-0398-5bca-9a48-b162d6138619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d67c9ae-1059-5bb6-951e-c7139ab21d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-123300" :question/problem "![](images/34/34b4b349b8295ee6443a85f019f6bf5e7ea6c6aafe9109ac7935610e6aefcacf.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}(25-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is three-quarters of the ellipse $36{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=900,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $5$ and $6,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=25-{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =25-(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =25-25{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =25(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =25{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(25-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }25{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2e9acc-3c64-51a7-9ab5-d350f9449ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387383} {:answer/id #uuid "89ac3f7c-fb26-5086-aea8-2db3445e4653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387384} {:answer/id #uuid "ef97d2f5-fde9-5e22-82b7-c60bd3fea467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387385} {:answer/id #uuid "ec794748-a3c8-58b6-8929-a9cf739d2848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{5t-3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387386} {:answer/id #uuid "26182461-9d9e-569f-b647-6e1cb1f93a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26182461-9d9e-569f-b647-6e1cb1f93a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387387} :answer-field/id #uuid "36154aca-9593-55ae-8c89-27fef1f72807" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "963025de-8b37-535e-a08b-0b16983c48ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-123387" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z},$ traversed along the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3i-2\\,j+3k$ and $b=5\\,i-j+7\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t[(5\\,i-j+7\\,k)-(3i-2\\,j+3k)] \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t(2i+\\,j+4k) \\\\  & =(2t+3)\\,i+(t-2)\\,j+(4t+3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+3,y=t-2,$ and $z=4t+3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+3)k \\\\  & =2i+j+4\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{2(2t+3)+(t-2)-(4t+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\cdot \\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45cba1d3-f8b7-5b76-9ef6-9eb975d2870d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387397} {:answer/id #uuid "95d593ff-808e-5dde-a3cc-592a9fac2bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387398} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c4989d-33d6-5c4f-b18e-27c12c50a4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{ }\\,\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387399} {:answer/id #uuid "79e5bff5-9c94-5976-bd20-8b6b92ba67c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387400} {:answer/id #uuid "319dbf5e-c635-5b66-abde-d87e62f4983b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79e5bff5-9c94-5976-bd20-8b6b92ba67c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387400} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b0a388b-3f2b-5b8c-b2b8-fb5efa3b5837" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6fb03672-f783-5453-94bf-11f27f84fe3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-123454" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta })^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34de18da-e1ca-5f8c-97df-2693296f5a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2},\\,-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387404} {:answer/id #uuid "aa27ce8e-bcaf-51a1-a29e-a8a4f48be71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\sqrt{2}-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387405} {:answer/id #uuid "21af6a56-2edf-5642-9e1f-191738f36d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,2-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387406} {:answer/id #uuid "372e4110-4717-5d1d-9f56-6b19027a531c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387407} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2bfecb-fc50-5b9d-8572-2f9284f9bdf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa27ce8e-bcaf-51a1-a29e-a8a4f48be71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\sqrt{2}-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387405} :answer-field/id #uuid "7066aadc-9ef8-5807-82c3-9874779befc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb8b3222-c045-5fd0-b252-08063208b15e" :question/math-academy-id "q-123455" :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}⟨\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡t}{1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}⟨\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡t}{1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡t}{1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t,{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1,\\,\\sqrt{2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\sqrt{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89718900-c739-578d-83f6-c38fc4e66b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x})=x+10{x}^{2}+15{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387652} {:answer/id #uuid "3a938509-73c2-5450-b23b-08b0f47d1535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x})=3x+\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387653} {:answer/id #uuid "e61a3117-c216-535e-a1d0-68ec22723659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x})=x+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{65{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387654} {:answer/id #uuid "f1dbfbd4-9506-5561-a115-232fa133f28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x})=5x+\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{65{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387655} {:answer/id #uuid "69eec5f1-cd49-5f67-af38-9dda6d7c7cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x})=5x+10{x}^{2}+15{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1dbfbd4-9506-5561-a115-232fa133f28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x})=5x+\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{65{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387655} :answer-field/id #uuid "00263cee-00a5-5ef9-8ce6-e02409517907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50adbbcc-971d-5bdf-b4fc-86bdd4f5bbae" :question/math-academy-id "q-125926" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the laws of logarithms, we observe that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ we recall the standard result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) & =x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo obtain the expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-4x),$ we replace $x$ with $-4x$ in the standard expansion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-4x) & =(-4x)-\\frac{(-4x)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(-4x)^{3}}{3}-⋯ \\\\  & =-4x-8{x}^{2}-\\frac{64{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, subtracting the expansion for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-4x)$ from the expansion for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-4x) \\\\  & =(x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯)-(-4x-8{x}^{2}-\\frac{64{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯) \\\\  & =(1+4)x+(-\\frac{1}{2}+8){x}^{2}+(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{64}{3}){x}^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =5x+\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{65{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a8f871e-102e-5e0f-90ec-e77fb3a35f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(1-y)}^{2}\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387694} {:answer/id #uuid "4e33660e-a885-5964-a557-f937bc374296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387695} {:answer/id #uuid "6815c23a-14ab-5690-8a70-dace2c92253f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387696} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b1f627-b094-579e-a196-f23dea135478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387697} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb869e-7968-5151-bf29-199efddadb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb869e-7968-5151-bf29-199efddadb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387698} :answer-field/id #uuid "693762ac-dad3-5029-98be-52f3f8bd2904" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e80b22b7-d962-5938-af93-f2a5f86bbdff" :question/math-academy-id "q-126615" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae26451a8f2962ed873fc5eb703197375bc1a02eff2240bd21cebac81ef26205.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}$ that lies to the right of the $y$-axis between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})=1-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(1-y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(1-2y+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\sqrt{2-2y+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09644c36-a1cc-56f1-a00d-a4df40d8e08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387715} {:answer/id #uuid "9c749662-7467-5a6f-b165-001eade9de92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387716} {:answer/id #uuid "7b543dea-6446-5902-a414-1793888be964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387717} {:answer/id #uuid "1c3f24ee-7b02-51e3-9459-5bd47def762b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387718} {:answer/id #uuid "a02f73f6-bb79-5897-b1e7-467f243e7234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09644c36-a1cc-56f1-a00d-a4df40d8e08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387715} :answer-field/id #uuid "945cca7f-88d6-5f7a-acdb-5fc30f1ee8b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcb08d10-5de4-50f1-acf0-54b821666e7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126715" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbdb10a20dd90cd0928e91851662fccaf8d0b03e86c44f58b5311ba805183e8e.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x+3$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+3 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-4x+3)=2x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+{(2x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-16x+17}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f9c523f-856e-54d0-90b2-a1b77c6d76c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{8}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387735} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4a42e9-dc29-5224-ae55-bb5a900e2cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{2}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387736} {:answer/id #uuid "04746f00-6fb0-58d6-a98f-2c5c6a3ee041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-\\frac{8}{3}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387737} {:answer/id #uuid "8b382dd1-3d19-557b-b49f-233151049ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387738} {:answer/id #uuid "d535518b-374a-5373-866f-536f3a3a2c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d535518b-374a-5373-866f-536f3a3a2c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387739} :answer-field/id #uuid "02851dff-4a2a-5216-a4ad-5a271323a251" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "193d7529-18a9-52a2-adf9-54c39fa112ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-126840" :question/problem "Find the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)\\,\\,\\,\\, & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =0+(-2)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{(0)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+\\frac{(8)}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193b4900-4d07-53e2-be4e-48d5ec8d43ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387798} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8b69e0-aa2b-5483-90dc-48d46fb9dab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387799} {:answer/id #uuid "f829b1b0-0a47-5ec0-9116-1383df3f1616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387800} {:answer/id #uuid "74a4e07c-290d-5499-a014-7387d054ab73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387801} {:answer/id #uuid "83f0b0aa-1ec1-5f8f-9713-262b37261532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f8b69e0-aa2b-5483-90dc-48d46fb9dab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387799} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f87249-b6b1-5efb-aa0a-6f8127d0855d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3540ef43-cb67-51c1-8423-cebf06f99cf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127221" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1693bed9-d0f0-55b1-b740-a527cbeb53ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+8{t}^{2}}}⟨2t,\\,2t,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387819} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a6ad77-6f89-543a-b273-f553d73a021e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387820} {:answer/id #uuid "3367f712-09e1-5ae1-8151-c97f6b2adc14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387821} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2576ba-96ef-5dd5-a88a-3c42695f8d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387822} {:answer/id #uuid "22a744b2-27a5-52ca-ad95-7d00b232171e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22a744b2-27a5-52ca-ad95-7d00b232171e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387823} :answer-field/id #uuid "b39d1a04-3d20-5a50-851c-2b9dd049f9b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9708eb7-d471-5841-8ae9-c412fc9cab47" :question/math-academy-id "q-127686" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,0,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4t}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(-2t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩}{(\\frac{2}{1+4{t}^{2}})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,0,2t⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0-0,-(1+4{t}^{2}),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0,\\,-(1+4{t}^{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be979d2e-0b17-5d83-a44b-e9e9f493f578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387826} {:answer/id #uuid "63d8e15a-36c0-5d0e-abb6-acb136a98a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387827} {:answer/id #uuid "d61781f9-0300-5886-be20-7da50554e568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3s+2),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s+2)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387828} {:answer/id #uuid "172c4425-fc97-5b38-be53-1268b3920119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387829} {:answer/id #uuid "be6d94df-309d-5d79-a0fb-6c489908600c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3s-6),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s-6)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be979d2e-0b17-5d83-a44b-e9e9f493f578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387826} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7434be1-bf74-5c28-a679-abb576a9da2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3223e0f6-e1e4-5997-b282-51a0c4f828e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-127689" :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(t)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=6t.$ Find $R(s),$ the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=6t,$ we have that $t=\\frac{s}{6}.$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $r(t)$ by its arc length $s,$ with $s\\ge 0,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{6}) \\\\  & =⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{s}{6}),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{s}{6})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb62c734-f04b-55c4-8bd3-c4793e629968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨9,0,16⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387833} {:answer/id #uuid "310bb578-398b-5e0c-b06f-4a951396331b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387834} {:answer/id #uuid "7198b6f9-5ef1-533d-9afe-6ea7aa3d2357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387835} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0ea389-fa08-5116-b82c-ba029d1888b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟨3,1,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387836} {:answer/id #uuid "c5225ba2-26d0-562f-a151-05f4fab8d07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨\\sqrt{3},1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7198b6f9-5ef1-533d-9afe-6ea7aa3d2357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387835} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ee013c-9eb7-52bb-bd68-12e344232014" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5d30766-fbf9-51ac-b32d-e1cc50064292" :question/math-academy-id "q-127709" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{3}{5}{t}^{3/2},\\,1,\\,\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{3/2}⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s={t}^{3/2},$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s={t}^{3/2},$ we have that $t={s}^{2/3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r({s}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{5}{({s}^{2/3})}^{3/2},\\,1,\\,\\frac{4}{5}{({s}^{2/3})}^{3/2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3s}{5},\\,1,\\,\\frac{4s}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parameterized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3s}{5}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4s}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{5},\\,0,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a731427-7b07-5f24-89c3-30874476c839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s,\\,3,\\,-2s\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387840} {:answer/id #uuid "10a6c696-58d2-5ef6-9472-8be6af11b238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s},\\,3,\\,\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387841} {:answer/id #uuid "1a627ef4-c24c-5b1b-bddf-6512cf41eb1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨2s\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s,\\,3,\\,2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387842} {:answer/id #uuid "e79af7ed-b39f-54ce-8e12-881ba9d9b1c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\frac{s}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,3,\\,\\frac{s}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387843} {:answer/id #uuid "2e26bff6-6ea0-5724-a3cc-0b6e736ef303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s},\\,3,\\,-\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a6c696-58d2-5ef6-9472-8be6af11b238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s},\\,3,\\,\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387841} :answer-field/id #uuid "68b00312-bec7-5cbc-886e-bfa4bdaf0060" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "792ae83b-6f8e-58bb-84d1-2c821da6f452" :question/math-academy-id "q-127723" :question/problem "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3,\\,t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =|t| \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},$ we have that $t=\\sqrt{2s}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt{2s}) \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s},\\,3,\\,\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6770c6c3-ca44-5901-927f-1f6eb9c2f483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,-\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387910} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f467ee-f10f-5e09-b08f-f8641934f5aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387911} {:answer/id #uuid "375265a8-c029-5e99-9d9a-45d5e93a7d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387912} {:answer/id #uuid "78262439-79b2-52fc-a07a-ed5fa9d567e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{t},-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387913} {:answer/id #uuid "b4369e5c-dffe-55df-9352-d2f0194bfc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-\\frac{t}{1+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "375265a8-c029-5e99-9d9a-45d5e93a7d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387912} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8592051-0273-5e7f-9289-65035199769c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00ec801b-e22a-5c19-899f-45b6911ab4a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-128570" :question/problem "If $g(u)=⟨\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+1),\\,1⟩$ and $u(t)=\\frac{1}{t}$, then $(g∘u)^{′}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(g∘u)^{′}(t)=g′(u(t))u′(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\frac{1}{u}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{u+1},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g∘u)^{′}(t) & =g′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(u(t))^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{u(t)+1},\\,0⟩\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{t}{1+t},\\,0⟩\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5228249-42b4-5b6c-a34b-688628cf3c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -\\frac{8}{3} & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388071} {:answer/id #uuid "0df908b7-b9cf-5d37-aca8-7f00b46a2051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388072} {:answer/id #uuid "afd3402b-d0f0-5d8e-92cd-42bc4ac1d793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388073} {:answer/id #uuid "269dae98-aadc-597a-82e7-371420d4533a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388074} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee9af5a-41eb-5f3b-a278-9fcb76973748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 9 & 0\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afd3402b-d0f0-5d8e-92cd-42bc4ac1d793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388073} :answer-field/id #uuid "95465894-f1f7-5a8d-84b8-f2ad025aafe5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f08a2c2-eb16-5d85-a714-beb36e4a0c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-130071" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 9 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ means that we need to multiply the third row of the matrix by $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the $3$rd row by $-\\frac{1}{3}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 9 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{3}:=(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 6 & (-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (-3) & (-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 9 & (-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 0\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -9 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59f4e581-a00c-5132-927c-1585f2f16335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388085} {:answer/id #uuid "a25cb316-03e0-5b80-b36c-bf23e9ecd400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{7}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388086} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce509f6-676c-5142-b2e8-6b75044a4ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388087} {:answer/id #uuid "56675fa5-8d6f-5214-9ce9-5265d8d72af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388088} {:answer/id #uuid "5bff0bff-8fcd-5363-b279-0ef233e06a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a25cb316-03e0-5b80-b36c-bf23e9ecd400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{7}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388086} :answer-field/id #uuid "368f7e81-de7f-51a5-98fc-f718da4aa1b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f930a915-2d68-50d6-9185-efb4889fde3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130209" :question/problem "At which point on the curve $2{x}^{2}=y+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i+j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-4x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i+j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (i+j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (i+j) & =0 \\\\ (-i-4x\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) & =0 \\\\ -1-4x & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ 2{(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & =y+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{7}{8}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf3b3e9-5525-5663-9cf2-a10207731431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{20}{{t}^{5}} \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388099} {:answer/id #uuid "6134b075-d637-5d00-8b92-595e8552ffba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{20}{{t}^{5}} \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388100} {:answer/id #uuid "5411e8cb-d9f4-526b-a3ba-970deb3f15d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4{t}^{5}} \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388101} {:answer/id #uuid "7825d2bf-aec1-5ba7-93a0-a00aa2285434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{20}{{t}^{5}} \\\\ {t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388102} {:answer/id #uuid "67b2892e-c95d-51f8-9994-b6d68c2ec8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{4{t}^{5}} \\\\ {t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cf3b3e9-5525-5663-9cf2-a10207731431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{20}{{t}^{5}} \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388099} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1b9e495-4e85-5dcd-86df-bc543e98d356" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "422634d2-16ff-5174-a2f8-b71facc9ec9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130400" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(t)$ given that $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{{t}^{4}} \\\\ {t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "We find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{{t}^{4}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{20}{{t}^{5}} \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49dad7e6-c04d-5a11-b68a-4dbee8c80d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388594} {:answer/id #uuid "1dda3dd3-1a0e-5252-9444-671ce40ac43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388595} {:answer/id #uuid "7a14bacb-16c0-5d24-bce5-8ff0db8979e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388596} {:answer/id #uuid "308d1266-b426-5761-89af-dbb5fb6c75fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388597} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8ab337-6e82-5011-8143-113f077d184e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "308d1266-b426-5761-89af-dbb5fb6c75fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388597} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de3ed7a-69f6-523c-afcd-9345a4e4c30b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b26a4f7-3d3e-5904-9022-e3e7b7a8173a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139033" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{2}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot |\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eab72eb-df6c-5a95-a802-c9f7ef1d2d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{5+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388601} {:answer/id #uuid "e523a055-2283-5b5c-9d35-196fa0e40859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(5t+1)^{2}\\sqrt{5+2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388602} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4ac22-92b5-57a3-afa1-1c5e224b3e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388603} {:answer/id #uuid "d95b283e-a098-55e5-a84f-cb7be24fa226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{25+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388604} {:answer/id #uuid "0779dd54-fc92-5681-9b18-2b2be2ca8c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}(5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d95b283e-a098-55e5-a84f-cb7be24fa226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{25+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388604} :answer-field/id #uuid "251babce-8907-527f-ae76-6475e4f67b0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d9271f7-51c3-50a5-a620-a2ed152f61bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-139067" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=5t+1,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+1)=5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(5)}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{25+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35450620-e1c1-5553-8d89-babffec49fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388794} {:answer/id #uuid "9509bf92-21de-58cf-a928-febc07388708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388795} {:answer/id #uuid "eba19a2d-d967-5a67-9c11-60ad3d49f2c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388796} {:answer/id #uuid "ab55f8c6-2adf-5f61-aac6-7bf5271da29d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388797} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe3e312-3a15-5ca5-b692-01ea036db64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe3e312-3a15-5ca5-b692-01ea036db64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388798} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bf69525-3508-5fe7-99c0-fbb77383cf15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2fb99b60-4344-5bc1-af82-6f45b3524b97" :question/math-academy-id "q-140911" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-(\\frac{4}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ea8228-51e6-5f85-b539-c1d15c0fe792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}-t-4)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388906} {:answer/id #uuid "860a0600-6c2c-5c9e-b5c4-9b17db189976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(t-4)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388907} {:answer/id #uuid "23c91797-5d7f-5e12-aa9e-092abce95ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{t}^{3}+t-8)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388908} {:answer/id #uuid "463a4d0d-9541-5db1-8cdc-bef6635f6bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+2t-8)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388909} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1c4954-271d-5c20-a752-fbf6f8daf6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+t-4)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b1c4954-271d-5c20-a752-fbf6f8daf6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+t-4)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388910} :answer-field/id #uuid "92df483e-25e7-5869-98dd-8c3813ab168b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7d9bec2-9225-5625-8a03-8734f22a69ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-148199" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-2y)\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=2-\\frac{x}{2}$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2-\\frac{t}{2}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2-\\frac{t}{2}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\frac{t}{2})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-2y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}-2(2-\\frac{t}{2})={t}^{3}+t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+t-4)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{3}+t-4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab06ac6-4b86-5107-beab-c84b66be2cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388913} {:answer/id #uuid "48d500af-45bd-5d76-b7e6-f7027293e1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388914} {:answer/id #uuid "eac27db1-c199-5099-9644-507bbdba7b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388915} {:answer/id #uuid "33e2affd-c422-5289-8198-ffb1637d1782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388916} {:answer/id #uuid "33ededb1-4f63-5237-92c5-029fc4f39566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48d500af-45bd-5d76-b7e6-f7027293e1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388914} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a5a001a-2ddf-5fd9-a315-06e7e52536bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d45e7151-1e23-5fc3-b354-b72fb9448af9" :question/math-academy-id "q-148205" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=2{e}^{x}$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2{e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+2{e}^{t}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc1884d7-f6fa-51d5-ab67-2d4dfe7971dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}+{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388941} {:answer/id #uuid "9df9f06f-758a-557e-a6d2-9c8187553a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{8}+2{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388942} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c21937-ce6c-561f-9c7f-beaefe07f762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{12}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388943} {:answer/id #uuid "29b74dcf-6de8-5e2b-a0c8-a8be7961cd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{4{x}^{6}+2{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388944} {:answer/id #uuid "ba847247-1fbc-5b03-a578-a9ed456b0bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba847247-1fbc-5b03-a578-a9ed456b0bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388945} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fd233a-e678-5701-92e3-fd27713cdc1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891e62c5-2f59-5791-9c12-b3816ce7c5d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15403" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{3/2}$ on $[0,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{9}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{3}{2})6{x}^{2}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{1/2}={x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}(2{x}^{3}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f97954b-fab9-555e-a63d-0b7ff6619a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/82557cc5a33963a7ec6d7910da8dc1409fd2cdc1245b00be009f7a237bee6c3a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389079} {:answer/id #uuid "4f425ff2-e50f-5b19-ab10-931b2a0a25ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389080} {:answer/id #uuid "35760d42-f2ba-5032-8da1-1ca1d76889e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/128955fddc503b24eb73c5a321a803a4318f16e83e88f1d28be0a0e621f926c9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389081} {:answer/id #uuid "fde379d9-5306-507a-a69f-f50ff3931c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b2026b5c41f64f72aa9d1c849f9357f4192860545e9a41241c2a0f87d33db6a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389082} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a07a20-8567-5538-9f06-4d6de140aa40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/709f9c571b81383cb436570ea4c63575a2fb7e83b1c2e377a6347f54c8ceea35.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f425ff2-e50f-5b19-ab10-931b2a0a25ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389080} :answer-field/id #uuid "79e6b72a-de31-55a4-8613-ca44de670c50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31d74e75-fb66-5eb8-9774-1cd2896eeaa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160909" :question/problem "![](images/95/951c3d3bca4116deebccbbabb5d7405bb07669cacb83c1e450204bb40c247d5c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f729c97f-3cdd-5808-9965-99a3a4b12cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/99/993c8929f019f9753f8b9e0b6662248bf9f41b8f743d423ef11c4838922de17c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389086} {:answer/id #uuid "338a9924-5265-5b6b-97fe-23afe4602a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/36/36cd7eaa8f2be8e3c8be883f7de52c2ea8bb87b2ac40b6ae0f9de22598d53ef6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389087} {:answer/id #uuid "2b492f1e-6fd0-5371-8d50-691cf2e7acb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ad/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389088} {:answer/id #uuid "0ee13b28-0f93-56ff-92a7-86287c2f6160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0c093c8b5b55bcf5c50b0fc6c60d47256c9aaf86b8114ba29d6cef5def8cd310.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389089} {:answer/id #uuid "9e45936a-1c95-55d8-9812-13920f362a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ce/ce5b81c795019bb779f766d70a42872ef15aac38440a4c38876c76842b3a0c69.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b492f1e-6fd0-5371-8d50-691cf2e7acb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ad/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389088} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dd89d67-b436-5e5f-8398-6f045a7d552b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62187b1b-2a1e-5c4c-8542-39321690d816" :question/math-academy-id "q-160912" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f436ee381cafdb41a4a8df2bc68c06751d7d0a6d55641303caf04dcb4cab6619.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=2$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ad/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65674cef-349e-53ba-9d3b-003cfa1ca839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/90571cebb5f46697204869a7dbd07e83036d4fe92a5f46045bf6279b3b307999.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389093} {:answer/id #uuid "06c89610-14b3-5554-aa9f-e51bce286bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e718cdc16345d0da40874901ed4dc276c5cf33a75949365b59129a7bb934a8c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389094} {:answer/id #uuid "1edeeca0-4ca4-5cf6-84ff-84ade5a12db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/10a9d70717e657082fd8c36dc98876fcea53297ce79b44585574efef821dba51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389095} {:answer/id #uuid "727029c5-1b11-518c-8047-594952cc6c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9d781ed8813991d36d3da4802efa94543f1ee3fc542e1b8c72ff7ad521974e58.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389096} {:answer/id #uuid "853b32d5-c84a-5ebe-8783-2a507609eb35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "853b32d5-c84a-5ebe-8783-2a507609eb35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389097} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ee8e133-63d6-556c-8c7e-bf88612f2e87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0aaf351a-049f-501d-8313-1944db65783b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160928" :question/problem "![](images/85/85e8422a0b3421db73d8cbe88ef3c51b59f628f233d48982840a21c4d26bd26f.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=4$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=4$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=4,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0345274-f2c4-5705-a32d-e15314701def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/ee80e889e142c56b48a884a71dd714349ffbb95a6bdee3a3406679ed1edfaaa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389114} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ace1d8-0fe6-5683-95d7-9cad14abffa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e50d218c70d9f4f5e77a9ef53b5fb2712b6b400450fc2165e7972b06621b9757.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389115} {:answer/id #uuid "04525aaf-6e10-5c32-b37f-1661336ebc54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389116} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf22586-b6de-5257-8078-913f559b060a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aafdff659d5c73ab37dfe9cf27c8777d03d7731f04dd106c149e6b4bfbc45fb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389117} {:answer/id #uuid "55b60f0e-fc18-56fe-b9cf-de221f61768b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/39/399dc3c9750ed7e041f1973cad0a24a18903360ba68f0dbcb42c566436c9e259.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0345274-f2c4-5705-a32d-e15314701def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/ee80e889e142c56b48a884a71dd714349ffbb95a6bdee3a3406679ed1edfaaa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389114} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f3b4e13-9ace-5415-af28-7000cb517d7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7dffa96-ac1f-5c9d-b16b-8ea8f986a8f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161025" :question/problem "![](images/de/de9f7a32a41bcecc1707b90d6818d58473473a4dc26d17e39e957461de95d671.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{6},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{12}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $\\sqrt{6}$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/ee80e889e142c56b48a884a71dd714349ffbb95a6bdee3a3406679ed1edfaaa3.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86459122-4bdd-512e-9e34-298272859e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389162} {:answer/id #uuid "20db1bb1-9d9a-5010-b5a7-6117b0a95c14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+y+xy}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389163} {:answer/id #uuid "1457b555-cd36-5515-a457-4a89ad7bf36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389164} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd6e4de-d5b0-5620-a4d8-71f31bfdbd8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}+2xy}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389165} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bd6ab0-9645-5100-a44f-fc36a1a0120c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}1+{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1457b555-cd36-5515-a457-4a89ad7bf36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389164} :answer-field/id #uuid "43daddd4-5f4f-51e5-ba06-33b9f5b182c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c26fb9d1-bd64-5afc-83db-47655a4115bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161138" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the surface $z=x{y}^{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y={x}^{2},$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=x{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/14/1419b067fe92945249655c5d29498aa4d3b0269d79ebd237fd9ed2a713b70b6a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})={y}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})=2xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{({y}^{2})}^{2}+(2xy)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5d540b0-4408-5667-a0db-a90347a718b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+(y+1){e}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389169} {:answer/id #uuid "37b76347-5859-5d6e-8b0f-ab07a22b98cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+(y+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389170} {:answer/id #uuid "d986df6b-5639-5102-8fd7-9a29abdd7669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389171} {:answer/id #uuid "86209031-deeb-5c6b-ae0a-144a5367c604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389172} {:answer/id #uuid "a63c61fd-3721-53ba-9d5b-95914b2da4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}1+{y}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d986df6b-5639-5102-8fd7-9a29abdd7669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389171} :answer-field/id #uuid "f23fcbc4-3abe-5fd3-bdce-8e4f9b6793af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc263672-04e3-59a9-9f18-ccb933eb5e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-161141" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the surface $z=y{e}^{x}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the lines $y=2x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=y{e}^{x},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/90/903b7f3c6bd9721a974160487ca5099fdf54ce02b61697a7830bb662b26d8953.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})=y{e}^{x}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(y{e}^{x})^{2}+({e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4c913e3-a5b7-538a-afbd-1307a15a3578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389376} {:answer/id #uuid "81a4450b-87b0-59da-a1ef-6435ca5d90ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389377} {:answer/id #uuid "e149b12c-7b25-524b-8729-94f1850ecae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389378} {:answer/id #uuid "eca24669-e860-58d9-b281-a8dfd9fad322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389379} {:answer/id #uuid "523a518d-0d02-5bce-83db-dc2c0add9e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eca24669-e860-58d9-b281-a8dfd9fad322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389379} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f75c73-bd47-59c3-b998-8d2856225676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b173a456-6d61-587a-a9bf-4d48c28e2049" :question/math-academy-id "q-162831" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+3y+2z$ on the intersection of the plane $2x-y-z=-1$ and the cylinder ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2x+3y+2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints  \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-y-z=-1,\\,h(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=-1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨0,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2\\lambda \\\\ 3 &=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 2 &=-\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda &=1 \\\\ y &=\\frac{2}{\\mu } \\\\ z &=\\frac{3}{2\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $y$ and $z$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,z=\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $y=\\frac{4}{5}$ and $z=\\frac{3}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y-z & =-1 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5} & =-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}. \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $y=-\\frac{4}{5}$ and $z=-\\frac{3}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y-z & =-1 \\\\ 2x-(-\\frac{4}{5})-(-\\frac{3}{5}) & =-1 \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{12}{5} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}),\\,(-\\frac{6}{5},-\\frac{4}{5},-\\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5})$ | $(-\\frac{6}{5},-\\frac{4}{5},-\\frac{3}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $4$ | $-6$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}),$ and its maximum value is $4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df3378a4-f098-5dd2-8ca5-d33c2875b948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389404} {:answer/id #uuid "ba36a6bb-461b-576b-a8b0-07e2ea3c3d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389405} {:answer/id #uuid "a27b4618-4b71-59b7-bacf-d0230094bfab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389406} {:answer/id #uuid "d29cf603-32b4-509c-97ca-bbaca8f5bc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389407} {:answer/id #uuid "aa56a467-d2f5-5753-b23e-6ee51008dcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa56a467-d2f5-5753-b23e-6ee51008dcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389408} :answer-field/id #uuid "090b72a8-f15b-5bfd-adae-9afc07d8a51e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6490b2e2-ebe2-52cf-9741-542b7f4cc483" :question/math-academy-id "q-163110" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))=\\frac{1}{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb4d826c-14ba-585c-aebd-1facb3368012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389451} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab762ed-0112-5d52-b62f-05b3b484b0b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389452} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa053a0-465d-5f0b-8742-c3a588a86e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389453} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6add72-368a-56d1-a701-8e8fbe0ceee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389454} {:answer/id #uuid "04400f5c-b01f-5a74-aa41-074c903d6463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb4d826c-14ba-585c-aebd-1facb3368012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389451} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc72668-2f1d-508f-943f-15caead3623c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d31a00a5-c404-50d8-b7f2-fda34cf2cc53" :question/math-academy-id "q-163685" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "226918d0-485c-57fb-b37f-551c971f4927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389465} {:answer/id #uuid "49371fc0-7c0a-5176-b84d-8fdac3f9bd9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389466} {:answer/id #uuid "4e01aaf7-2644-59d0-beea-cbe8e4f810f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389467} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec4407e-4e50-5120-b041-d1dbb0df353d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389468} {:answer/id #uuid "13bff32a-258c-58a1-a056-0f3af9f9b3f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e01aaf7-2644-59d0-beea-cbe8e4f810f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389467} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2247e4-31f8-5907-8437-cf47cd59d1d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f633b7bf-41b1-55e5-94fa-734d0d539fd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163711" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{1}{6}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}({1}^{3})=\\frac{1}{6}\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=\\frac{1}{6}({2}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90ca48dc-9ebd-5ddb-b0fd-4e9e4bf3e523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389472} {:answer/id #uuid "bb17431a-f15d-57b4-8155-d193fd52d02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389473} {:answer/id #uuid "1341ffcc-a761-51c7-8882-f9f94717f134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389474} {:answer/id #uuid "760e497b-11bf-5399-b595-62bbffc2cba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389475} {:answer/id #uuid "4f138711-dd5f-59c8-bf2b-72bb46435286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f138711-dd5f-59c8-bf2b-72bb46435286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389476} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0ec5fc-0f7c-5acb-8a13-4ea7ffbac1a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c495a30-23c5-530b-9d91-e188ad9ee72e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163714" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $\\frac{8}{5}\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $3t\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{3}{2}({0}^{2})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\frac{3}{2}({1}^{2})=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=\\frac{3}{2}({2}^{2})=6\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {6}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{5}-\\frac{9}{5} \\\\  & =27\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f162c583-fe62-5fba-9c96-68bf896663bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389492} {:answer/id #uuid "64cf8fe1-66d9-5ab3-8251-577ddc7a7669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389493} {:answer/id #uuid "9d91dbe3-c6c4-5402-85f2-5aebe1228361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389494} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2b9d9e-a55d-5269-90e7-268bfa853936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389495} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ba81b1-55ae-50e5-9a14-d540f92d66ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d91dbe3-c6c4-5402-85f2-5aebe1228361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389494} :answer-field/id #uuid "525e80e6-2970-531f-b670-bb348fa022c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "677a6071-7ce4-5abc-a513-660a8ad8e603" :question/math-academy-id "q-164142" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{4}=\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{-1+1}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(0,1)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[3{x}^{2}y+(y-1)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{x}^{2}+2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[2{x}^{3}+2x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(2+2-(-2-2)) \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =1+{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1+9 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c30d2a3-e06b-50c4-99ad-bf1c23c38c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389499} {:answer/id #uuid "da5e3918-bbb8-54d5-8d14-12ed1ce8da12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389500} {:answer/id #uuid "d978a27c-4cb5-55a0-92a9-74eda2d5f58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389501} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e5818b-5846-5d89-a311-73ed695522ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389502} {:answer/id #uuid "44ad420e-5cbd-5f25-8e01-134e784e36c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c30d2a3-e06b-50c4-99ad-bf1c23c38c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389499} :answer-field/id #uuid "647cfa17-f0ad-5abd-9877-f316086b5810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb6e2375-c7b1-5f37-b50b-167a66de4a06" :question/math-academy-id "q-164147" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$\nand has mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=2x,$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nGiven that the center of mass is at $(2,1),$ find the moment of inertia about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}((x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x(x-2)^{2}+2x(y-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2x(x-2)^{2}y+\\frac{2}{3}x(y-1)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4x(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4{x}^{3}-16{x}^{2}+16x+\\frac{4}{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4{x}^{3}-16{x}^{2}+\\frac{52}{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{4}-\\frac{16}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{26}{3}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =81-144+78-0 \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =15+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =15+4 \\\\  & =19\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389581 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e91d68c4-e924-5c0b-8892-337b390a9278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/cadd20667d93ddc9058703331d2442573b4febc0eb79b0c50749490a859d7006.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389583} {:answer/id #uuid "d252b9b0-032b-5d42-9ac1-9f37846b1397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/44/4442c3b03668bf3d4b8090c56c4ad083e85aa4399a0326255fe2322f2dcc729b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389584} {:answer/id #uuid "d19a1858-cafb-517d-ba52-283fda091cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/779c8384569a3cf6cec79186e69a657b4e23f98f2dd464a3879700b4c08fd27a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389585} {:answer/id #uuid "b9429aad-0aa1-50e2-95f4-b8ee60295084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b934a5fcadb46619e6c3631e6f9d78c8a0f72beeb023f9696c01422503188586.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389586} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9ba8eb-d544-5202-91c3-233917e73327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/eed323df68b293af1fa8ec575d4dca23f16fe01865b01c3fa9817eead7b66fd2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d19a1858-cafb-517d-ba52-283fda091cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/779c8384569a3cf6cec79186e69a657b4e23f98f2dd464a3879700b4c08fd27a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389585} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba892752-d68e-5265-b5cc-644accea6428" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f2385ee-c24f-5a4c-9712-f5717ae06cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-167668" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/e9/e992d80b1245e6fad1c1e2968c396c51ef863d56d36c184592f8025fc450fa99.png)\n\n- Reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/aa/aa0778c338e9b57aca82057edc1b984e051d05a3f5413c2b1c70c3874fcf063e.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $3$ units down to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,-4)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/25/25b4ef6f489f19d81b1d54a3240faf5e99f20a9463aa2ce9bd2e39796bf39cb3.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4933cab4-6c6f-5e1e-b9e9-375a48b8bbd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c7ef699169e521845d96c6b28392271c27cca116b449277c66817dc2dbb6491.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390342} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd7f842-ec45-57a8-93f4-8edce627ac5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/3357b2cf67b40afa54b081c67855f28a74d71209459382c0e421b6e2da7d114f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390343} {:answer/id #uuid "c26f47ff-1aa3-50e2-840e-6408b7c4640f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dcec9bbf7c5a1badb7e0193a16e802fe0b5558c1a076f65da60db5a24a58f667.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390344} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0bbddd-f61f-57da-9b31-1e4e5fbfbe90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04899589bc5527f5c35bae7d2b8564141ea5ae49f0aab1f500e57be7d854cc05.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390345} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdb4a02-ebd9-56ca-9cfc-e2db41cc39a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d3d7ea5224600381e2dd4f62618ce8a60804f89bb6949b82989017aa2556f87a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b0bbddd-f61f-57da-9b31-1e4e5fbfbe90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04899589bc5527f5c35bae7d2b8564141ea5ae49f0aab1f500e57be7d854cc05.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390345} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff039e87-4af3-51fe-96a4-b9dddf2e393f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09d1de29-e974-591a-b590-9887d91abf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-18523" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/4d/4dacecba4626f7c202777cad0a723295a2954f4b0c65fde83a81c6c849414d51.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,2),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/b7/b747ae8c29f01b840930d81f9c4b706d049ca685ee982efff717ddb13213937d.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd564d9f-63fb-590f-bd58-4b804a9ab1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390355} {:answer/id #uuid "a0809cc0-8245-5717-8ded-90aaf0006a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390356} {:answer/id #uuid "de905a14-40b4-54a2-9966-938904217d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390357} {:answer/id #uuid "e79926dc-55a2-51bb-946e-9f9ee21d2e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x-3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390358} {:answer/id #uuid "703b11b7-a06a-596e-9766-2f893cbd71bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd564d9f-63fb-590f-bd58-4b804a9ab1e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390355} :answer-field/id #uuid "4529ba01-864b-583e-ae2f-75a351bb79bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db8ef128-2717-5091-8870-85aa35ff95e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-19532" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+35 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+({6}^{2}-{6}^{2})+35 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-12x+36)-36+35 \\\\  & =(x-6)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x=10\\int \\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-6.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x-6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5145dde8-60d2-54b7-ad8d-2892ff5d3963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4161037f51b8c4d95037c3ff28d7e6afecb354d4e0fabc22e5dd819bb65b6cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390533} {:answer/id #uuid "6680ac81-034b-5243-a46f-e14b15ca391f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4511ffac1899bdeb512b0b50b50b6a121477f486ef7e2efc83697fd9dddb8d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390534} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7b451c-b477-571c-b8f0-b6b471ddf99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/953bb642696a6b9c934627f978d150d27adbc7f73779a3d017c2ba714ff0835e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390535} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd2d67a-323e-5832-a842-a36b0a6fce73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d6034af21a31fdfdbaff6760ab5dd97bb227d0538a475aef9e5c14a1559e16b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390536} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbecea5-be5b-5a1e-bd55-ff4ec8267187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df2b0819dabd55db60af06fecb52e19a013b471ab632484d4066ef5e011ccfe5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fbecea5-be5b-5a1e-bd55-ff4ec8267187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df2b0819dabd55db60af06fecb52e19a013b471ab632484d4066ef5e011ccfe5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390537} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe628ffa-6fae-5342-9742-f9cf2475f662" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4cf19722-da75-5d66-8864-81b9e6bc4f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-27477" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/f0/f02afa6dd2d1c107fd72b1221fddb7f6c698506deb834cad0dc11af1bebb18e6.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $\\frac{1}{10}$ units down. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=-\\frac{1}{10},$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{9}{10}).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57539870ff45bae449d3ad7c11580d9c8068cfe76ac98c2f849ee38494288036.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11991a9d-264d-57e7-9fdd-da7348694651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/77ddeefbcbd24c80f6ed0bb7a29d2a43e3151436b0a60b56686b1c67ca31c738.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390540} {:answer/id #uuid "f32fd0b0-49ce-539d-9672-8d020e261fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f73be44a90c4a240b316bcf7ac5586b54ed1ca4cdedfac42f055cd39086ffe2d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390541} {:answer/id #uuid "407fad6c-67c3-5a63-bb5b-a49d0b7c8b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f405cfe76e1d69d7ecad35e96ced1c76c1b64130a118d881d331bcb0b119068c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390542} {:answer/id #uuid "a9519e6c-79f2-5f62-a983-2670eb2a7aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/33f78e0590a7039f0e2e17d5bc871e0503dd3526d282b5fb76e4ef0f3b78cc28.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390543} {:answer/id #uuid "29d4d4e8-fe84-544d-8606-3bfce7839133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9d332fa8d3d8ef65daaf34bf906c5ca7cd7e2edaf562386ed4e0ebc4555b0a64.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29d4d4e8-fe84-544d-8606-3bfce7839133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9d332fa8d3d8ef65daaf34bf906c5ca7cd7e2edaf562386ed4e0ebc4555b0a64.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390544} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a74683-a726-5cb6-b2c2-b253e72cf78d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5e7ffcf-d49d-5dfe-9c73-ca9c0369ba7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-27538" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y=3{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}-1?$" :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/4c/4cdc9e4fa78513bca01fa10242ba6c3f12b31c262448a8edf350c76e84b188b8.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=3{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $3.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,3)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/8e/8e7d6c5eb931f55a9f94ac33382c0e1ec9e9e49f50b3ab538d9848d6a77be665.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=3{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}-1.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,3)$ to $(0,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-1.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d332fa8d3d8ef65daaf34bf906c5ca7cd7e2edaf562386ed4e0ebc4555b0a64.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f70e31a-a6d8-57be-8930-fe9e46798cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e545a84145ac0a139ee5ecdf9692c20837d211f9a70fd79ad33dd13f60c856f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390547} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd43261-0c67-5dec-abd5-e030f8c694d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f6/f63e49a2e8f4a0702841eb132093bf6f69b8405af25719ea8f5abbf2bd691122.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390548} {:answer/id #uuid "c08648ea-dc6d-5578-89fa-a0be944fb62c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c64e79b7c0069aaf7d4393a6879612f58f48103247b1ba7efef3531dc3986b93.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390549} {:answer/id #uuid "84311dad-8f82-5f9c-ab3a-b304e0b27aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/571e2268e6b7f41e80b71c8764d321c298fe58232e5b4c5a84ce309fa0dd3530.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390550} {:answer/id #uuid "8b546f29-cf64-542e-8f37-af2b900bcda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d7af2718ba7c30956218a00da51e77db950183bd2a85dbe74107e493fd5f03b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f70e31a-a6d8-57be-8930-fe9e46798cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e545a84145ac0a139ee5ecdf9692c20837d211f9a70fd79ad33dd13f60c856f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390547} :answer-field/id #uuid "b29207c3-5e76-521f-8c52-fed97e9cf151" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab17c05b-3a04-5ac4-9de0-6246c2badd83" :question/math-academy-id "q-27540" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/e6/e648e3a5f0c844d6596d4848629c3e6749647228116bcc5da6fce250895141d5.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/37/378016bbd17371e3dfee1e80934e76b1357139d405cb320ac5befa252da82b28.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ to $(0,-\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/e5/e545a84145ac0a139ee5ecdf9692c20837d211f9a70fd79ad33dd13f60c856f7.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21c9bca-5889-5378-95f5-dc19174253a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390587} {:answer/id #uuid "5637088d-2110-5861-a9e4-5418d97814d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390588} {:answer/id #uuid "98625043-9024-52ca-9b27-4d102460e169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390589} {:answer/id #uuid "a3127733-14dd-544e-9920-fd0e17095202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390590} {:answer/id #uuid "510c63c8-8440-585e-ac63-db0b2b6359b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98625043-9024-52ca-9b27-4d102460e169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390589} :answer-field/id #uuid "c557a89c-b4c6-5e67-988d-6c588aefa937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23dbd8da-da31-5df7-bb91-fad8d1a7bceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-29182" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={30}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={30}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/8c/8c6e3ae6bdbfb1c66aee719f77225453992fffe6f3c6e3dfdc3f1ff92c59863a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={330}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required dommain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={330}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{30}^{∘},-{330}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-30\\cdot (-330)=9\\,900\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c95a3fca-97ff-5e46-ad67-42c5b5b68ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bc4c0c21c45fc3ed02d88150facf89718896a3e2992105f5a8a5d89b0355f9e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390594} {:answer/id #uuid "73326b1a-1067-5996-991e-bb9d853a4e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6edb90966ce90a141e3fa4ee1a9eb3cf9c7131e3121e45f56b16bc4395076d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390595} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a74885-a855-53a6-a12b-5c1f9db59688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/062274f402396a61c76fae3a47c54de80ad31deac970c746f3f8f4627ce535f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390596} {:answer/id #uuid "dbeafe1a-8a14-5f3a-8d70-6e398d0ebc6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b3/b3f96cf981a9c127dd0e3c0fa3b73f548db8c640bbc4f0e048b4576356b69532.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390597} {:answer/id #uuid "44eec2a2-c1e5-5830-a6f1-3345cbd3b0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b48f88ac53107fddece776c81d91fc5684c7d6f06ea3b862fb99cd973eae2dc3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73326b1a-1067-5996-991e-bb9d853a4e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6edb90966ce90a141e3fa4ee1a9eb3cf9c7131e3121e45f56b16bc4395076d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390595} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e7cf18-216c-59ac-8255-427849d7152c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6a50d86-9c2e-59fc-b26e-b4043bba0c39" :question/math-academy-id "q-31146" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}-4?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/e2/e2f13711ca62a8a4a698e23bdfe78caca6df44d88ded27b88c517219dffbb866.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $4$ units down. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=-4,$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,-3).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}-4$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc7f93220ac18ac5671b7b41de141940ca5296139b836d56755fdccf6164ed26.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b59601-a89e-5e1d-a669-b2b7a7e8029b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/50/50a0caebc732fec06957fadef2326f89cfef7f94d278e2ecc2774ad5168c0374.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390668} {:answer/id #uuid "f12c77ec-9cb6-5bae-a7a4-e6c90735d0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c653557a459c6fd4989737d418e22e642aeed4e03193ea08c8d55dd43ca9ede8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390669} {:answer/id #uuid "492d6d67-62e0-56d1-8a29-7869d43f0e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ef/efbb630d38c907b1ce053bd5fa9a14b305d4392b4b573cd294348f7360d2d327.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390670} {:answer/id #uuid "5d556352-d259-5a3a-b309-08f3051bd6ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56ed5bc3bf1dd167086f6057a7c39b070055c375b9d8c9e316ac987fca404a98.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390671} {:answer/id #uuid "ee06efd6-1e71-5a6b-9560-a51a9c6f979f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/11b9c82d9bc0503ea3d17d8acbe9d50f3d6a8648dd2405dcaa42bf8667b121ca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d556352-d259-5a3a-b309-08f3051bd6ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56ed5bc3bf1dd167086f6057a7c39b070055c375b9d8c9e316ac987fca404a98.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390671} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f412805-45f5-5bfb-b60d-78aecc7af701" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8208cfa-7eb3-58e5-ba4e-3c6c7623b96c" :question/math-academy-id "q-317355" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/c9/c925aea9381d9ecb008b7ab609ef72817a7c8bc51a5e3ab5abf4fb33f9055730.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,-4)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/fa/fa429123677e529f376c871642e0c4c0986cb5ea5319f7bf59b225ea0d36f792.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $3$ units down to give $y=-4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3.$ The point $(0,-4)$ moves to $(0,-7)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/35/3511221e88625a10cc41b1a1470c13c38ef9398afb88fa09ae2f033e88ad9696.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aea8813-2274-5a0e-900a-f3cb9e331517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d8c6d83734b0e8e8b227c66a20db2092ebe1530bc20831d500515e0785561cd9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390675} {:answer/id #uuid "c6aab864-1ea7-581f-bbab-1705433414fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b1/b186111aafb5